One aspect of science is that models can be disproved by
experiment.  Belief doesn't enter into it.  Believe something
all you want, like the Earth is flat, but no amount of belief is
ever going to make that belief true when the experiments start.

Because save-scumming works in so very many other types
of games, it is natural to believe that save-scumming will help
with Nethack.  No matter how obvious this is to a newbie and
how firmly they believe it, actual experimentation reveals that
in Nethack it's false.

Simply put, save-scumming is disliked because it leads down
a road of false hope based on belief not compatible with
experimental result knowledge.  People who save-scum do not
advance in skills the way they think they do.  It's a trap that is
made by the player in a game that already has so many traps
made by the DevTeam.

At first I save-scummed in the false belief that I was improving
my skills to the point I would be able to ascend.  In fact, what
I was doing was practicing melee combat to the exclusion of
other strategies and failing to learn lessons from each game.
Eventually I relented, accepted that experimental data beats
belief, and stopped save-scumming.  I started trying to learn a
lesson from each game.  I am now a far better player than I was
years ago.

The reason save-scumming fails seems to be the level of
complexity of the game.  Maybe that theory is true, maybe not.
There isn't the data available for that.  What data that is available
is simple -Ascenders don't save-scum and save-scummers stop
ascending when they join tournaments where it can't be done.

